/// Defines a line of sight ray in a world inertial or body frame of reference. Defines a line of sight ray in a world inertial or body frame of reference.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub struct LosVariableAType {
    /// Specifies whether the sibling Azimuth and Elevation elements are referenced to a body, NED, or inertial frame. Specifies whether the sibling Azimuth and Elevation elements are referenced to a body, NED, or inertial frame.
    #[serde(rename = "Reference")]
    pub reference: LosReferenceEnum,
    /// Indicates the azimuth from an observation point to a point of interest. When the sibling Reference element indicates INERTIAL, azimuth is defined as the angle between two lines in a global horizontal plane at the observation point. The first line is from the observation point to true north. The second line is from the observation point to the point of interest projected onto the global horizontal plane. An azimuth of zero indicates true north with values increasing clockwise. When the sibling Reference element indicates BODY, azimuth is defined as the angle between two lines in the plane containing the observer's roll and pitch axis (the "RP" plane). The first line is from the observation point along the observer's roll axis or "down the nose" in aircraft terminology. The second line is from the observation point to the point of interest projected onto the RP plane. An azimuth of zero indicates directly ahead of the observer ("down the nose") with values increasing clockwise. An azimuth of Pi/2 indicates directly right of the observer ("down the right wing"). The terms azimuth and bearing are often used interchangeably but they are technically different. Azimuth values are angles between 0 and 2*Pi radians (0 and 360 degrees) relative to reference. Bearing values are a combination of a reference direction (north or south) and an angle between 0 and Pi radians (0 and 180 degrees) towards the east or west direction. For example, an azimuth of Pi/2 radians (90 degrees) is equivalent to a bearing of "North 90 East" or "South 90 East". UCI uses azimuth because it is easier for machine/software use. Indicates the azimuth from an observation point to a point of interest. When the sibling Reference element indicates INERTIAL, azimuth is defined as the angle between two lines in a global horizontal plane at the observation point. The first line is from the observation point to true north. The second line is from the observation point to the point of interest projected onto the global horizontal plane. An azimuth of zero indicates true north with values increasing clockwise. When the sibling Reference element indicates BODY, azimuth is defined as the angle between two lines in the plane containing the observer's roll and pitch axis (the "RP" plane). The first line is from the observation point along the observer's roll axis or "down the nose" in aircraft terminology. The second line is from the observation point to the point of interest projected onto the RP plane. An azimuth of zero indicates directly ahead of the observer ("down the nose") with values increasing clockwise. An azimuth of Pi/2 indicates directly right of the observer ("down the right wing"). The terms azimuth and bearing are often used interchangeably but they are technically different. Azimuth values are angles between 0 and 2*Pi radians (0 and 360 degrees) relative to reference. Bearing values are a combination of a reference direction (north or south) and an angle between 0 and Pi radians (0 and 180 degrees) towards the east or west direction. For example, an azimuth of Pi/2 radians (90 degrees) is equivalent to a bearing of "North 90 East" or "South 90 East". UCI uses azimuth because it is easier for machine/software use.
    #[serde(rename = "Azimuth")]
    pub azimuth: f64,
    /// Indicates the elevation from an observation point to a point of interest. When the sibling Reference element indicates INERTIAL, elevation is defined as the angle between two lines in a global vertical plane at the observation point. The first line originates from the observation point and extends along the azimuth. The second line is from the observation point to the point of interest. An elevation of zero indicates the point of interest is in the observation point's horizontal plane. Elevation values are positive when the point of interest is above the horizontal plane, negative when below. When the sibling Reference element indicates BODY, elevation is defined as the angle between two lines in a plane containing the observer's yaw axis. The first line originates from the observation point and extends along the azimuth. The second line is from the observation point to the point of interest. An elevation of zero indicates the point of interest is in the observer's roll-pitch (RP) plane. Elevation values are positive when the point of interest is above the RP plane, negative when below. Indicates the elevation from an observation point to a point of interest. When the sibling Reference element indicates INERTIAL, elevation is defined as the angle between two lines in a global vertical plane at the observation point. The first line originates from the observation point and extends along the azimuth. The second line is from the observation point to the point of interest. An elevation of zero indicates the point of interest is in the observation point's horizontal plane. Elevation values are positive when the point of interest is above the horizontal plane, negative when below. When the sibling Reference element indicates BODY, elevation is defined as the angle between two lines in a plane containing the observer's yaw axis. The first line originates from the observation point and extends along the azimuth. The second line is from the observation point to the point of interest. An elevation of zero indicates the point of interest is in the observer's roll-pitch (RP) plane. Elevation values are positive when the point of interest is above the RP plane, negative when below.
    #[serde(rename = "Elevation")]
    pub elevation: f64,
}

